{:edb/db-before-t 1775 :edb/db-after-t 1776 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535088 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:36:48.392Z" 13194139535088 true] [17592186114982 1100 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" 13194139535088 true] [17592186114982 1100 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) =0$\n2. The system $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $A^{-1}$ does not exist" 13194139535088 false] [17592186114982 1145 "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186114982 1145 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139535088 false] [17592186114983 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139535088 true] [17592186114983 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n3. The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139535088 false] [17592186114983 1145 "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/e49dcc022775cc8eb5317611a3fae4d21892a7c566989f67f65e5213ee5c6b9d.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186114983 1145 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$det(A)|= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot k - (-2) \\cdot (-2); = k - 4$,\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4$. So, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$.\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139535088 false] [17592186114984 1100 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" 13194139535088 true] [17592186114984 1100 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$.*\n\n1. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $\\det(A) = 0$" 13194139535088 false] [17592186114984 1145 "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139535088 true] [17592186114984 1145 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139535088 false] [17592186115154 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330492 13194139535088 true] [17592186115154 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330520 13194139535088 true] [17592186115154 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330547 13194139535088 true] [17592186115154 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330554 13194139535088 true] [17592186115155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330499 13194139535088 true] [17592186115155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330533 13194139535088 true] [17592186115155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330561 13194139535088 true] [17592186115155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330568 13194139535088 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330506 13194139535088 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330513 13194139535088 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330540 13194139535088 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330575 13194139535088 true] [17592186115653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186113318 13194139535088 false] [17592186115653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330527 13194139535088 true] [17592186115653 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535088 true] [17592186115653 1100 "Consider the lines $2x + y = 2$ and $x + ky = 5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\mid __________$.*" 13194139535088 false] [17592186115653 1100 "Consider the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" 13194139535088 true] [17592186115653 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible.*" 13194139535088 true] [17592186115662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535088 true] [17592186115662 1100 "Let\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $det(A) = 0$\n2. $Ax = b$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\cdot 1$ column vector $b$\n3. $A^{-1}$ does **not** exist" 13194139535088 false] [17592186115662 1100 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" 13194139535088 true] [17592186115662 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186115722 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535088 true] [17592186115722 1100 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax = b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is invertible\n2. $det(A) \\ne 0$\n3. $Ax = 0$ has an infinite number of solutions" 13194139535088 false] [17592186115722 1100 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" 13194139535088 true] [17592186115722 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330493 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1089 "q-127918" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1100 "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1113 #uuid "a109e6d7-dc35-5583-9b59-06b02bed5042" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330492 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330494 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330495 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330496 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330497 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330498 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1041 "selection" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330493 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330498 13194139535088 true] [1759