 lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330568 1113 #uuid "33d9b6a8-08d4-5b01-9855-80084a55a511" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330568 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330570 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330571 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330572 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330573 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330574 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1041 "selection" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330571 13194139535088 true] [17592186330569 1110 #uuid "9be3d716-4ca8-5fb8-9fa7-226b3f320ec2" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330570 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330570 1111 #uuid "5377ee4b-8a29-54b8-b75e-795f2259686b" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330571 1034 "I only" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330571 1111 #uuid "954f2c77-f11b-56f9-beca-340af0d23e75" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330572 1034 "III only" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330572 1111 #uuid "6c98df9a-31f5-55e1-a09f-4f4cd8df24dd" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330573 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330573 1111 #uuid "0e86979e-0170-5764-ba6d-bb6f6fb8ed76" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330574 1034 "II only" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330574 1111 #uuid "36764740-bc96-512d-8b60-fa6648ebf46f" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330576 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1089 "q-73596" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1100 "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+2y=1 \\\\ -3x+ky=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true: \n\nIf the system's coefficient matrix is invertible then $\\text{__________}\\,.$" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1113 #uuid "3aa6bf3f-a787-587a-a396-0aad162cd33d" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330575 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible then $det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330577 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330578 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330579 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330580 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330581 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1041 "selection" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330578 13194139535088 true] [17592186330576 1110 #uuid "eed586c5-6075-5923-ba00-ca02b5cddd8d" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330577 1034 "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  has infinite solutions" 13194139535088 true] [17592186330577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535088 true] [17592186330577 1111 #uuid "eff50924-30cf-58ef-b6b1-c